// Practice quiz: Accessibility basics
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/html-and-css/accessibility-basics

::o1 decorative image::A page shows a decorative swirl between two paragraphs. Which markup is right for it? {
=An img with an empty alt attribute, so screen readers skip it #Correct. An empty alt marks a purely decorative image, which assistive technology can then ignore.
~An img with alt\="decorative swirl image" #Not quite. Describing decoration makes screen reader users listen to noise; decoration should be skipped.
~An img with no alt attribute at all #Not quite. Without alt, a screen reader may read the file name instead of skipping the image.
~An img with alt\="image" #Not quite. That adds meaningless noise; an empty alt is the signal for decoration.
}

::o1 keyboard trap::Which situation is a keyboard trap? {
=Focus moves into an embedded map, and neither Tab nor Escape moves it out again #Correct. A keyboard trap is any part of the page that keyboard focus can enter but not leave.
~A link opens a new page when Enter is pressed #Not quite. That is normal link behavior.
~Tab moves through the controls in a different order from the visual layout #Not quite. That is a focus order problem, but focus can still move on.
~A button has no visible text #Not quite. That is a missing name, not a trap.
}

::o2 role versus element::A developer adds role\="checkbox" to a div so it is announced as a checkbox. What else must they build by hand that a native checkbox input already gives? {
=Keyboard focus, Space to toggle, and keeping its checked state up to date #Correct. ARIA roles describe an element but add no behavior, so focus, keys and state all need extra code.
~Nothing, the role makes the browser provide the behavior #Not quite. ARIA roles do not cause browsers to provide keyboard behavior.
~Only a CSS style that makes it look like a checkbox #Not quite. Looks are the smallest part; focus, keys and state are missing too.
~An alt attribute on the div #Not quite. alt belongs on images and does not add behavior.
}

::o2 native first::When should you choose a native HTML element instead of an ARIA role? {
=Whenever a native element already gives the behavior and meaning you need #Correct. A native element brings focus, keyboard behavior and the role together; ARIA is for gaps HTML cannot fill.
~Never, because ARIA roles are more modern #Not quite. ARIA only describes; it does not replace native behavior.
~Only on mobile pages #Not quite. The reason is behavior and meaning, on every device.
~Only when the page has no JavaScript #Not quite. Even with scripts, rebuilding native behavior by hand is extra work and easy to get wrong.
}

::o3 keyboard check::You test a page with the keyboard alone. Which result fails the check? {
=A Filter control works with a mouse click, but Tab never reaches it #Correct. Every interactive control must be reachable and operable from the keyboard.
~Tab reaches a button and Space presses it #Not quite. That is exactly what should happen.
~Shift+Tab moves focus back to the previous link #Not quite. Moving back with Shift+Tab is expected.
~A focused link shows a clear outline #Not quite. A visible focus indicator is a pass, not a failure.
}
